// (0.26.3) `volec fix --only alloc`: allocation as a capability
// (front/check/alloc_rules.vl, docs/grammar.md "Allocators"). The exact
// fixes of the allocator rules' errors, in rounds (a fix lets the checker
// see the next: a struct made generic over its allocator, then its uses):
//
//   alloc.default    `Vec<u8>` becomes `Vec<u8, Gpa>`, what the 0.26.2
//                    default meant; refused in an item generic over an
//                    allocator (`A` may be meant), but for the program's
//                    own type in its generic impl (the impl's `A`); an
//                    impl block without its type's allocator gets it
//                    (`impl<T, A: Allocator> Bag<T, A>`); a declared
//                    default (`A: Allocator = Gpa`) is taken out
//   alloc.no_in      `Vec::new()` becomes `Vec::new_in(gpa)` (`collect()`,
//                    `collect_in(gpa)`): the one allocator value in scope
//                    (`gpa` first), passed last; refused when there is
//                    none or several
//   alloc.field      a struct or enum holding an allocator its parameters
//                    do not name is made generic over it (`struct P<A:
//                    Allocator> { v: Vec<u8, A> }`; one it has already
//                    from an earlier round is reused); its uses then name
//                    it (alloc.default, the next round); refused when the
//                    allocator is held through another type
//   alloc.safe_impl, alloc.static, alloc.last: no fix (no tool writes a
//                    SAFETY comment; the value moves into main; `volec
//                    refactor signature` moves a parameter)
//
// The errors without a fix are left, each with its reason.

use crate::base::{buf_new, buf_str};

// The codes whose exact fixes `--only alloc` makes, as `--codes` takes
// them (`,C,` pieces).
pub fn alloc_codes() -> i64 {
    let b = buf_new();
    buf_str(b, c",alloc.default,alloc.no_in,alloc.field,");
    b
}
